{"name":"Back in Black","hex":"#16141c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"back","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["At or near the rear.","Returned or restored to a previous place or condition."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["To or in a previous condition or place.","In a direction opposite to that in which someone or something is facing or normally pointing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/back"},{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"}],"entries":[{"title":"Back in Black","blocks":[{"text":"Back in Black is the seventh studio album by Australian rock band AC/DC, released on 25 July 1980, by Albert Productions and Atlantic Records. It was the band's first album to feature Brian Johnson as lead singer, following the death of their previous vocalist Bon Scott. After the commercial breakthrough of their 1979 album Highway to Hell, AC/DC was planning to record a follow-up, but in February 1980, Scott died from alcohol poisoning after a night out in London. The remaining members of the group considered disbanding, but ultimately chose to continue on and recruited Johnson, who had previously been the vocalist for Geordie."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/AC-DC_-_Back_In_Black_%28original_1980_album_cover_with_embossed_lettering%29.jpg/330px-AC-DC_-_Back_In_Black_%28original_1980_album_cover_with_embossed_lettering%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_in_Black","wikidata":"Q155715"},{"title":"Back in Black (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Back in Black\" is a song by Australian rock band AC/DC. It was released as the second US single from their seventh album, also named Back in Black, and released in 1980 through Atlantic Records. Notable for its opening guitar riff, the song was written as a tribute to the band's former singer Bon Scott, who died suddenly in February 1980. In 1981, it reached number 37 on the Billboard Hot 100. In 2012, it reached number 65 in Australia and number 27 in the United Kingdom. It has been widely regarded as one of the best rock songs in history."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Back_in_black_by_acdc_US_7-inch_single_side-A.png/330px-Back_in_black_by_acdc_US_7-inch_single_side-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_in_Black_(song)","wikidata":"Q2466691"},{"title":"Human back","blocks":[{"text":"The human back, also called the dorsum, is the large posterior area of the human body, rising from the top of the buttocks to the back of the neck. It is the surface of the body opposite from the chest and the abdomen. The vertebral column runs the length of the back and creates a central area of recession. The breadth of the back is created by the shoulders at the top and the pelvis at the bottom."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Posterior_view_of_human_female_and_male_backs_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_back","wikidata":"Q133279"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Grenoble79_01.jpg/500px-Grenoble79_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Grenoble79_01.jpg/500px-Grenoble79_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bon Scott, the band's former vocalist, in December 1979","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGrenoble79_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg/500px-Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg/1280px-Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg/500px-Brian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Back in Black was the first AC/DC album to feature Brian Johnson (pictured in 1982) as lead singer.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrian_Johnson_-_Manchester_Apollo_-_1982.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Beaches_in_Nassau%2C_Bahamas.jpg/500px-Beaches_in_Nassau%2C_Bahamas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Beaches_in_Nassau%2C_Bahamas.jpg/500px-Beaches_in_Nassau%2C_Bahamas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Back in Black was recorded in Nassau, the capital of The Bahamas","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeaches_in_Nassau%2C_Bahamas.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/MuseReading270817-54.jpg/500px-MuseReading270817-54.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/MuseReading270817-54.jpg/1280px-MuseReading270817-54.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/MuseReading270817-54.jpg/500px-MuseReading270817-54.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Brian Johnson performing \"Back in Black\" alongside Muse (guitarist Matt Bellamy pictured) at the 2017 Reading Festival","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseReading270817-54.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/250px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/Gray204.png/120px-Gray204.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray204.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Back_Muscles.jpg/120px-Back_Muscles.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik